content.text:
The page snapshot shows the DASHBOARD IS VISIBLE (the "Tablero" button is there). But `ensureAuthenticated` failed because `dashboard.isVisible()` returned false at the time it checked, and then the login form wasn't visible.

So the issue is a RACE: `ensureAuthenticated` does:
```js
await page.goto('/', { waitUntil: 'domcontentloaded', timeout: 60_000 });
const dashboard = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Tablero' }).first();
if (!(await dashboard.isVisible().catch(() => false))) {
  const emailField = page.getByLabel('Correo electrónico');
  await expect(emailField).toBeVisible({ timeout: 30_000 });
  ...
}
```

`isVisible()` does NOT wait — it's an immediate check. If the app hasn't hydrated/rendered the sidebar yet (domcontentloaded fires early), `isVisible()` returns false, and then the code waits for the LOGIN form, which never appears because the user IS authenticated.

That's a REAL BUG in the shared helper — a race condition. The fix: wait for EITHER the dashboard OR the login form, then branch.

Let me rewrite `ensureAuthenticated`:

```ts
export async function ensureAuthenticated(page: Page) {
  await page.goto('/', { waitUntil: 'domcontentloaded', timeout: 60_000 });

  const dashboard = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Tablero' }).first();
  const emailField = page.getByLabel('Correo electrónico');

  // La app puede renderizar la barra lateral (sesión activa) o el formulario
  // de acceso. `isVisible()` no espera, así que se espera a que aparezca
  // cualquiera de los dos antes de decidir.
  await expect(dashboard.or(emailField).first()).toBeVisible({ timeout: 60_000 });

  if (await dashboard.isVisible()) {
    return;
  }

  await emailField.fill(ADMIN_EMAIL);
  await page.getByLabel('Contraseña', { exact: true }).fill(ADMIN_PASSWORD);
  await page.getByRole('button', { name: /Iniciar Sesión/i }).click();
  await expect(dashboard).toBeVisible({ timeout: 30_000 });
}
```

Wait — but `dashboard.or(emailField)` — `or()` requires both to be on the same page; it returns a locator matching either. `.first()` on that gives the first match in DOM order. That works for `toBeVisible`.

Hmm, but there's a subtlety: if the login form is showing, `emailField` is visible. If authenticated, `dashboard` is visible. Good.

But wait: there's another subtlety. The page might show the LoadingScreen first (neither visible) and then resolve. `expect(...).toBeVisible()` waits. Good.

Another subtlety: the "Tablero" button — is it in the mobile sidebar (hidden) and the desktop sidebar (hidden on small screens)? At the default Playwright viewport (1280x720), the desktop sidebar is visible (`md:flex`). Let me check: the aside is `hidden ... md:flex`. At 1280px width, `md:` (768px) applies → visible. Good.

Hmm, but actually: `<aside className="fixed bottom-0 left-0 top-0 z-50 hidden w-[280px] ... md:flex flex-col ...">`. Yes visible at 1280.

Now, this race explains a LOT of the flaky failures! Many tests failed with "waiting for getByRole('button', { name: 'Tablero' })" style errors.

Actually wait — but most tests passed. The race only bites when the app is slow to hydrate. With 4 workers against a warm dev server, it can happen.

This is a significant fix. Let me apply it.

Also: the login button name — the app uses "Iniciar Sesión" (with accent) per LoginForm. The old qa-project-lifecycle used 'Iniciar sesión' (lowercase s) which would still match case-insensitively (getByRole name is case-insensitive substring). Good.

Let me now update `e2e/testCredentials.ts`.

Also, one more important consideration: `page.getByLabel('Contraseña', { exact: true })` — the password input. Let me verify LoginForm has label "Contraseña".

